ngm:bitcoin-proof-of-work-protocol-etf-custody a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Bitcoin ETF custody is the institutional safekeeping of the bitcoin backing a spot exchange-traded fund, performed by qualified custodians under regulatory and audit requirements. It typically uses cold storage with multi-signature or multi-party-computation key management, segregation of client assets, insurance, and proof-of-reserves attestation. This custody model bridges traditional regulated finance and on-chain asset control, and is central to the integrity of spot Bitcoin ETFs."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin ETF Custody"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:digital-asset ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-established ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.72"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:multi-sig-governance ;
    vc:slug "bitcoin-etf-custody" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "blockchain" .
ngm:bitcoin-proof-of-work-protocol-fungible-token-protocols a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Runes and Glyphs refers to two complementary Bitcoin token protocols: Runes, which encodes fungible token balances directly in Bitcoin transaction outputs using OP_RETURN data, and Glyphs, a related protocol developed by Melvin Carvalho that encodes token metadata as on-chain inscriptions compatible with the Ordinals framework. Together they extend Bitcoin's base layer with native fungible asset issuance without requiring a separate blockchain, enabling DAOs, project tokens, and programmable digital objects on Bitcoin testnet and mainnet."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin Fungible Token Protocols"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:software-engineering ;
    vc:enables ngm:asset-tokenisation ;
    vc:enables ngm:digital-asset ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-emerging ;
    vc:isPartOf ngm:blockchain ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.35"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:decentralised-autonomous-organisation ;
    vc:slug "bitcoin-fungible-token-protocols" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "infrastructure" ;
    vc:uses ngm:digital-objects ;
    vc:uses ngm:nft .
ngm:bitcoin-proof-of-work-protocol-halving a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "The Bitcoin halving is a protocol-enforced event, occurring approximately every 210,000 blocks (roughly four years), at which the block subsidy paid to miners is cut in half. It implements Bitcoin's disinflationary monetary policy, capping total supply at 21 million coins and progressively reducing new issuance until it reaches zero around the year 2140. Halvings have historically preceded major shifts in mining economics and market price, and they are central to Bitcoin's narrative as a scarce, predictably issued digital asset."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin Halving"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:mechanism-design ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-established ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.8"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:bitcoin-proof-of-work-protocol ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:mining-pool ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:proof-of-work ;
    vc:slug "bitcoin-halving" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "blockchain" .
ngm:bitcoin-proof-of-work-protocol-improvement-proposals a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Bitcoin Improvement Proposals (BIPs) are the formal design documents through which changes, enhancements, and new features are proposed and ratified for the Bitcoin protocol and its broader ecosystem. First introduced by Amir Taaki in 2011, modelled on Python's PEP and Python Enhancement Proposal process, BIPs serve as the primary coordination mechanism for a decentralised developer community that lacks any central authority. Each BIP progresses through a defined lifecycle — draft, proposed, final, and optionally superseded — and must achieve rough consensus among economic nodes, miners, and users before activation. BIPs are organised into three tracks: Standards Track (protocol-level changes requiring network-wide adoption), Informational (guidelines and best practices), and Process (procedural rules governing the BIP process itself)."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Bitcoin Improvement Proposals"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:blockchain-governance ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r3130 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r3131 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r3132 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r3133 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r3134 ;
