ngm:customer-due-diligence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Customer due diligence (CDD) is the set of regulated procedures by which a financial institution or obliged entity identifies and verifies a customer, understands the nature and purpose of the business relationship, and assesses the money-laundering and terrorist-financing risk it poses. It encompasses identity verification, beneficial-ownership identification, screening against sanctions and politically exposed person lists, and ongoing monitoring of transactions and risk profile. CDD is a core obligation of anti-money-laundering regimes, calibrated through a risk-based approach that escalates to enhanced due diligence for higher-risk customers and permits simplified measures for lower-risk ones."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Customer Due Diligence"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:kyc ;
