ngm:data-flow-diagram a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A data flow diagram (DFD) is a visual representation of how data moves through a system, showing processes, data stores, external entities, and the trust boundaries between them. In security practice it is the standard input artefact for threat modelling, since attack surfaces and applicable threats are identified by walking each flow and boundary crossing in the diagram. It is a structural tool rather than a behavioural one, deliberately omitting control flow and timing to keep the focus on where data moves and where it is trusted less."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Data Flow Diagram"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:threat-modelling ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-established ;
    vc:isPartOf ngm:threat-modelling ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.6"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:slug "data-flow-diagram" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "security" .
ngm:data-flow-mapping a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Data Flow Mapping"@en .
ngm:data-format a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A Data Format is a formal structural and encoding specification that governs how raw bytes are organised, typed, and interpreted when data is stored, transmitted, or exchanged between computational systems. Formats encode decisions about byte ordering, field delimitation, schema evolution, compression, and type systems, ranging from binary serialisation protocols (Protocol Buffers, Apache Avro, MessagePack) to human-readable interchange formats (JSON, XML, YAML, TOML) and domain-specific schemas (DICOM for medical imaging, glTF for 3D assets, FITS for astronomical data). The choice of data format critically determines interoperability scope, parsing overhead, storage efficiency, schema evolution capability, and compatibility with downstream processing pipelines and analytics engines."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Data Format"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:data-management ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r6860 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r6861 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r6862 ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r6863 ;
