ngm:discrete-cosine-transform a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "The discrete cosine transform, DCT, is an integral transform, closely related to the discrete Fourier transform, that expresses a finite sequence of data points as a sum of cosine functions oscillating at different frequencies, concentrating most signal energy into a small number of low-frequency coefficients. This energy-compaction property makes it the core building block of lossy image and video codecs such as JPEG and MPEG, which quantise and discard high-frequency coefficients to achieve compression. Video codecs apply the DCT, or block-based variants of it, to spatial blocks of pixel data before entropy coding."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Discrete Cosine Transform"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:fourier-transform ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-draft ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:slug "discrete-cosine-transform" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "infrastructure" .
ngm:discrete-event-simulation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Discrete event simulation is a modelling technique that represents a system as a sequence of distinct events occurring at specific points in time, advancing the simulation clock directly from one event to the next rather than in fixed time steps. Each event triggers state changes and may schedule further events, typically processed in timestamp order via a priority queue. It is widely used to model queuing systems, logistics networks, and multi-agent interactions where continuous-time simulation would be computationally wasteful."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Discrete Event Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:simulation ;
    vc:enables ngm:agent-based-modelling ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-emerging ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.5"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:slug "discrete-event-simulation" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "artificial-intelligence" ;
    vc:uses ngm:priority-queue .
