ngm:economic-competitiveness a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Economic competitiveness is the capacity of a firm, sector or economy to sustain productivity, innovation and market share relative to peers, typically assessed through measures such as productivity growth, export performance and capacity to attract investment and talent. In the context of artificial intelligence, it refers to how AI adoption and capability affect a firm's or nation's relative economic standing. Investment in AI capability and competitive dynamics within AI markets are both treated as drivers of broader economic competitiveness."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Economic Competitiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:national-competitiveness ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-established ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.65"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:national-competitiveness ;
    vc:slug "economic-competitiveness" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "artificial-intelligence" .
ngm:economic-development a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Economic development is the sustained process of improving a region's productive capacity, living standards, and institutional capabilities, typically measured through indicators such as employment, infrastructure investment, and income growth. It is pursued through policy instruments including industrial strategy, financial inclusion programmes, and public investment in skills and infrastructure. National and regional strategies increasingly treat technology adoption as a lever for economic development."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Economic Development"@en ;
