ngm:elliptic-curve-diffie-hellman a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) is a key-agreement protocol in which two parties each combine their own private elliptic-curve key with the other party's public key to derive an identical shared secret, without transmitting that secret over the network. It provides the same forward-secrecy properties as classical Diffie-Hellman key exchange but with much smaller key sizes for equivalent security, because it relies on the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem. It is the key-exchange mechanism used in TLS 1.3 and other modern TLS-based encryption to establish ephemeral session keys."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:elliptic-curve-cryptography ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-draft ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.55"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:diffie-hellman-key-exchange ;
    vc:slug "elliptic-curve-diffie-hellman" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "security" ;
    vc:uses ngm:elliptic-curve-cryptography .
ngm:elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm-problem a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm Problem"@en .
ngm:elliptic-curve-group a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "An elliptic curve group is the algebraic structure formed by the points on an elliptic curve defined over a finite field, together with a geometrically defined point-addition operation that satisfies the group axioms. The apparent difficulty of the discrete logarithm problem within this group, recovering a scalar multiplier from a known point and its scalar multiple, is the hard mathematical problem that elliptic curve cryptography relies on for security. Elliptic curve groups underpin cryptographic primitives such as Schnorr signatures and Pedersen commitments used in blockchain systems."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Elliptic Curve Group"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:elliptic-curve-cryptography ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-emerging ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.0"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:slug "elliptic-curve-group" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "security" .
