ngm:ergodic-theory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Ergodic theory is the branch of mathematics that studies the long-term statistical behaviour of dynamical systems that preserve a measure. Its central result, the ergodic theorem, gives conditions under which the time average of a quantity along a single trajectory equals its average over the whole state space. The theory provides the foundations for understanding when sampling a process over time yields the same information as sampling its underlying distribution, which is essential for Monte Carlo methods and statistical learning."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Ergodic Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:dynamical-systems ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:mathematics ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:probability-theory ;
