ngm:flp-impossibility a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "The FLP Impossibility (Fischer, Lynch, Paterson 1985) is a foundational theorem in distributed computing proving that no deterministic protocol can solve the [[Consensus]] problem in a fully asynchronous message-passing system if even one process may crash. The result arises because message delays are unbounded, making it impossible to distinguish a crashed process from a slow one, so any protocol that always terminates can be manipulated into a state of permanent indecision. Consequently, every practical [[Consensus Algorithm]] must relax at least one of the FLP assumptions — typically by introducing partial synchrony, randomisation, or probabilistic termination."@en ;
    rdfs:label "FLP Impossibility"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:distributed-consensus ;
    vc:bridgesTo ngm:blockchain-consensus ;
    vc:bridgesTo ngm:smart-contract-execution ;
    vc:contrastsWith ngm:cap-theorem ;
