ngm:implicit-neural-representation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "An Implicit Neural Representation (INR) is a method of encoding continuous signals—such as 3D shapes, scenes, or images—as the weights of a neural network rather than as discrete grids or meshes. The network acts as a function that maps spatial or temporal coordinates to signal values, enabling theoretically infinite resolution. INRs are widely used in novel-view synthesis, shape reconstruction, and physics simulation."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Implicit Neural Representation"@en ;
