ngm:linearizability a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Linearizability is a strong consistency model for concurrent and distributed systems requiring that every operation appears to take effect atomically at a single point in time between its invocation and its response, consistent with a global real-time ordering. It is a composable (local) property: a system is linearizable if each individual object is linearizable. Informally it guarantees that once a write completes, all subsequent reads observe that write or a later one, giving the illusion of a single, instantaneous copy of the data. Formalised by Herlihy and Wing, it is the gold standard against which weaker models such as eventual consistency are contrasted."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Linearizability"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:consistency-model ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r13510 ;
