ngm:low-latency-networking a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Low-latency networking is the design and operation of network architectures that minimise the round-trip delay between communicating endpoints, typically targeting single-digit or sub-millisecond latencies. It combines edge placement, optimised transport protocols, traffic prioritisation and predictable routing to support interactive and time-critical workloads. In spatial computing it underpins responsive immersive experiences where perceptual lag must remain below human-detectable thresholds."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Low-Latency Networking"@en ;
