ngm:phishing-resistance a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Phishing resistance is a property of an authentication mechanism whereby credentials cannot be captured, replayed, or relayed by an attacker impersonating a legitimate service, because the authentication protocol cryptographically binds the exchange to the origin requesting it. Mechanisms such as FIDO2/WebAuthn and hardware security keys achieve this by verifying the requesting origin as part of the cryptographic challenge, so a spoofed site cannot obtain a usable credential. It is increasingly mandated as a baseline requirement for high-assurance authentication standards."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Phishing Resistance"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:authentication ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-draft ;
    vc:isPartOf ngm:authentication ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.55"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:slug "phishing-resistance" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "security" .
ngm:phishing-resistant-authentication a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Phishing-resistant authentication is a class of authentication methods designed so that credentials cannot be captured and replayed by an attacker who tricks a user into interacting with a fraudulent site or relay. It achieves this primarily through public-key cryptography combined with origin binding, so that a credential is cryptographically tied to the legitimate service's domain and will not authenticate to an impostor. FIDO2/WebAuthn passkeys and hardware security keys are the canonical implementations, replacing shared secrets such as passwords and one-time codes that remain vulnerable to interception."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Phishing Resistant Authentication"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:authentication ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r16387 ;
