ngm:postquantum-cryptography a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Postquantum Cryptography (PQC) is the class of cryptographic algorithms designed to remain secure against adversaries equipped with large-scale quantum computers, replacing public-key schemes such as RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography that are broken by Shor's algorithm. PQC families include lattice-based, hash-based, code-based, and isogeny-based constructions, several of which NIST standardised in 2024 (e.g. CRYSTALS-Kyber, CRYSTALS-Dilithium)."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Postquantum Cryptography"@en ;
