ngm:radio-access-network a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A radio access network (RAN) is the part of a mobile telecommunications system that connects user devices to the core network over the air interface via base stations and antennas. It manages radio resource allocation, modulation, and handover across cells. Modern architectures such as Open RAN disaggregate hardware and software to enable multi-vendor, virtualised deployments."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Radio Access Network"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:network-infrastructure ;
    vc:hasMaturity ngmi:maturity-established ;
    vc:qualityScore "0.72"^^xsd:float ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:physical-layer ;
    vc:relatedTo ngm:wireless-radio ;
    vc:slug "radio-access-network" ;
    vc:sourceDomain "infrastructure" .
ngm:radio-frequency a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Radio Frequency (RF) refers to the portion of the electromagnetic spectrum, roughly 3 kHz to 300 GHz, used to transmit information wirelessly by modulating alternating current onto an antenna. It is the physical-layer medium underlying cellular networks, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and low-power wide-area networks such as LoRa. RF characteristics, including frequency band, transmit power, and propagation behaviour, determine a wireless technology's range, bandwidth, and power consumption trade-offs."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Radio Frequency"@en ;
