ngm:sampling-based-planning a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Sampling-based planning is a family of motion-planning methods that find feasible paths by randomly sampling the configuration space and connecting collision-free samples into a graph or tree, rather than constructing an explicit representation of the free space. By trading completeness for probabilistic completeness, these methods scale to the high-dimensional spaces typical of articulated robots. Representative algorithms include the Probabilistic Roadmap and the Rapidly Exploring Random Tree."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Sampling Based Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:motion-planning ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r18727 ;
