ngm:wide-area-network a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A wide area network (WAN) is a telecommunications network that interconnects sites across large geographic distances — cities, countries, or continents — typically by carrying traffic over links leased from or operated by carriers rather than infrastructure the user owns end to end. WANs join local area networks into a single reachable whole using technologies ranging from leased lines, MPLS, and carrier Ethernet to broadband internet, cellular, and satellite, with the internet itself the largest example, and they trade the high bandwidth and low latency of the LAN for reach, at recurring circuit cost."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Wide Area Network"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:networking-infrastructure ;
    rdfs:subClassOf _:r22498 ;
